{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2176-10-26 01:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good evening. I’m Dr. Lee. You came in with chest pain earlier today. Can you tell me more about what happened?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2176-10-26 01:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes… it started this morning. A squeezing feeling in the middle of my chest. It went away with Tylenol, but then came back around 5 PM. I called EMS."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2176-10-26 01:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. Did the pain go anywhere else? Like your arms, neck, or back?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2176-10-26 01:20:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, it went up to both shoulders. I also felt dizzy and short of breath. The ambulance gave me aspirin and two nitroglycerin pills under the tongue. That helped."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2176-10-26 01:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. This feels different from your prior heart attack, you said?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2176-10-26 01:23:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yeah, not as crushing. But it scared me. And lately, I’ve been having chest pain when I walk or climb stairs. It’s been happening more often."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2176-10-26 01:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s important. We also noticed your blood sugar was high when you arrived—408. You have diabetes, correct?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2176-10-26 01:26:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I take insulin twice a day—Lantus in the morning and at night. And I use Lispro when my sugar is high."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2176-10-26 01:28:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We gave you some insulin in the ER to bring it down. Your heart tests—like EKG and blood markers—were normal. No sign of a heart attack this time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2176-10-26 01:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not my heart? But I’ve had stents before… in the right coronary artery."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2176-10-26 01:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You do have known heart disease. But your recent stress test was normal, and you haven’t had more chest pain since arriving. So we don’t think you need another procedure right now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2176-10-26 01:34:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about the nitroglycerin I took? Should I keep using it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2176-10-26 01:35:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, if the pain comes back, use it as prescribed. We’re also starting you on a new medication—isosorbide mononitrate—once a day to help prevent angina."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2176-10-26 12:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your blood sugar is better now. We gave you your usual insulin dose. How are you feeling?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2176-10-26 12:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Much better. No chest pain. But I’ve also been having some burning when I pee and flank pain. And a cough with mucus."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2176-10-26 12:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Thanks for mentioning that. We’ll check your urine and lungs. But for now, your heart is stable. We increased your blood pressure medicine—lisinopril—to 5 mg daily, and your statin to 10 mg."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2176-10-26 12:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. And I can go home today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2176-10-26 12:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, if all stays well. You’ll continue Plavix and aspirin, keep using insulin, and restart metformin. Follow up with your cardiologist and primary doctor soon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2176-10-26 12:25:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And if the chest pain comes back?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2176-10-26 12:26:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Use nitroglycerin. If it doesn’t go away in 5 minutes, call 911. Same if you have shortness of breath, nausea, or arm pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2176-10-26 12:28:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. Thank you, Doctor."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2176-10-26 12:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll get your discharge papers ready. Rest well, and take care of yourself."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2176-10-26 13:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just a quick update—your labs look stable. No signs of infection in your urine yet, but we’ll make sure you’re checked at follow-up. All set for discharge."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2176-10-26 13:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I’ll keep an eye on things and call if anything changes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2176-10-26 13:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. And keep managing your diabetes—those sugars need to stay down to protect your heart and kidneys."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2176-10-26 13:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I know. I’ll try harder. Maybe I need to adjust my diet."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2176-10-26 13:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That would help. Consider talking to a diabetes educator. We can set that up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2176-10-26 13:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’d appreciate that. Thanks again."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2176-10-26 00:28:00",
    "admission_end": "2176-10-26 18:50:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 60-year-old woman with a history of coronary artery disease, diabetes, hypertension, and hyperlipidemia was admitted for recurrent substernal chest pain radiating to both shoulders, associated with dizziness and shortness of breath. The pain resolved with sublingual nitroglycerin and aspirin in the field. Initial evaluation showed hyperglycemia (glucose 408) but no acute ischemic changes on EKG or elevated cardiac enzymes, ruling out myocardial infarction. She had increasing exertional angina over recent months but remained stable during admission. Her medications were adjusted: lisinopril and simvastatin doses increased, metoprolol switched back to succinate formulation, and isosorbide mononitrate started for angina prophylaxis. Metformin was held initially but restarted at discharge. She also reported dysuria, flank pain, and productive cough, which were noted for outpatient follow-up. She was discharged home in stable condition with instructions to monitor symptoms and follow up with her primary and cardiac care providers.",
